  "title": "Protect the App private key from agent and gate reads, at the daemon's startup",
  "file": "src/cli/daemon.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "The operator stores the App private key outside the protected config directory. An agent running as the same user can read that key because its sandbox policy does not deny the path, potentially allowing App impersonation.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test \"$TMPDIR/limitless-846-adjudication.test.ts\"",
    "output": "F1 {\"kind\":\"external\",\"configuredRegistered\":false,\"canonicalRegistered\":false,\"agentDeniesConfigured\":false,\"agentDeniesCanonical\":false}\nF1 {\"kind\":\"symlink\",\"configuredRegistered\":false,\"canonicalRegistered\":false,\"agentDeniesConfigured\":false,\"agentDeniesCanonical\":false}"
   },
   "evidence": "The throwaway probe invoked startDaemon with mocked listeners and inspected protection before integrations mounted. HEAD registers only worker files: `for (const file of files) privateFiles.push(protectPrivateFile(join(home, file)));`, then starts workers and integrations without registering githubApp.privateKeyPath. homeReadPolicy incorporates `privateReadPaths()` into its denials. Base has the same startup loop but no App-key configuration; HEAD introduces the new secret without connecting it to protection. The probe establishes missing generated denials; an actual confined agent read was not attempted. GitHub App configuration is restart-required under config-reload.ts, so live key-path replacement does not require additional handling."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An operator stores the App private key outside the config directory, for example at ~/keys/app.pem, which the new GUIDE text explicitly allows ('or an absolute file path'). Editor agents run as the same user and are blocked only from a fixed list of credential paths, so a model working on a run can read the key and act as the App on GitHub.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: proving that an agent can actually read the file needs a real Seatbelt-confined agent. The gap is visible in the code: nothing ever adds the key path to the private read paths that agent read policies deny.",
   "evidence": "At head, src/cli/daemon.ts:50-58 protects only `worker-tls/key.pem` and the files under `worker/` through `protectPrivateFile`, and nothing registers `cfg.githubApp.privateKeyPath`. src/harness/read-policy.ts:148-190 builds the editors' `denyRead` from `.config/limitless`, `LIMITLESS_CONFIG_DIR`, configured `deny_read` (which includes configDir) and `privateReadPaths()`. For non-gate editors (`gate=false`) the policy returns only `denyRead`, and the rest of the home stays readable. src/config.ts resolves `private_key_path` with `resolve(configDir, a.private_key_path)`, so an absolute path outside the config dir is accepted, and docs/GUIDE.md says 'relative to the config directory, or an absolute file path'. A key inside the config dir is covered; one anywhere else is not."
  }
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "title": "Never let a flag's value select the identity: pass the known repository explicitly",
  "file": "src/integrations/github-identity.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A PR author sets the description to exactly '--repo'. When the factory merges it, the description changes identity selection, causing the merge to use the owner's credentials despite the repository being configured for the App.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test \"$TMPDIR/limitless-846-adjudication.test.ts\"",
    "output": "F2 {\"outcome\":\"merged\",\"calls\":[{\"operation\":\"view\",\"selected\":\"o/r\",\"appToken\":true},{\"operation\":\"merge\",\"selected\":\"--match-head-commit\",\"appToken\":false}]}"
   },
   "evidence": "The probe used the actual mergePullRequest helper with a fake token endpoint and mocked process execution; no GitHub request or real merge occurred. HEAD parses identity with `args.findIndex((a) => a === \"--repo\" || a === \"-R\")` and returns `args[flag + 1]`, without distinguishing flag values. mergePullRequest constructs `['--subject', title, '--body', body, '--match-head-commit', sha]`. A body equal to '--repo' therefore selects '--match-head-commit' as the repository, and `!repos.has(repo.toLowerCase())` returns the owner identity. Base used sh directly and had no per-repository identity parser."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Someone edits a factory PR on an App-listed repository so that its body is exactly '--repo' (or '-R'). When the factory squash-merges it, the repository guess reads the next flag as the repository name and the merge runs silently under the owner's login instead of the App, against the 'never fall back to the owner' rule.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/adj/p.ts  (calls githubRepo on the exact argument list mergePullRequest builds, with body '--repo'; then with bodies containing '--repo x/y' or another repo's URL)",
    "output": "--match-head-commit\no/r\no/r\no/r"
   },
   "evidence": "src/integrations/github-identity.ts:106-108: `const flag = args.findIndex((a) => a === \"--repo\" || a === \"-R\"); if (flag >= 0) return args[flag + 1];` matches any argument equal to '--repo', including a flag's value. src/git/repos.ts builds `[\"--subject\", title, \"--body\", body, \"--match-head-commit\", sha]` from GitHub's PR body (`data.body`) and calls `githubSh(cmd, \u2026)` without an explicit repository. 'o/r' is not a listed repo name the provider knows, so it chooses the owner. Only an exact '--repo' or '-R' value triggers this: bodies that merely contain those strings or a URL are harmless, because the PR URL comes first."
  }
 }
